  "title": "fix(integrations): keep newer context when refreshes overlap",
  "body": "## Summary\n\nTwo saved summaries for the same project can start overlapping context refreshes. If the earlier request finishes after the later one, Cursor and Grok Build currently replace the newer rules-file snapshot with the older response. The next session then reads stale context.\n\nThis adds a shared publication guard for overlapping refreshes of the same rules file within one worker process. A refresh advances the published sequence only after its synchronous file write succeeds. HTTP errors, empty responses, and write failures still allow an earlier usable result to be published. Per-file counters are removed when the last request finishes.\n\nBoth integrations use the same guard. Existing Grok registration/file checks remain in place, and the worker bundle and source map are rebuilt. This handles in-process refresh ordering; it does not add cross-process file arbitration or change project-exclusion semantics.\n\n## Validation\n\n- A real loopback HTTP fixture drives both public update functions in isolated processes and inspects the rules files on disk. Two- and three-request reverse completion reproduce **4 failures on the original implementation**, with **10 passing controls**.\n- All **14 HTTP scenarios pass** after the fix, including normal completion order, HTTP 503, empty content, a real rename/write failure, independent workspaces, and a subsequent refresh after overlapping requests finish.\n- `bun test tests/integration/context-refresh-order.test.ts tests/integration/grok-build-rules.test.ts tests/cursor-registry.test.ts`: **43 passed**.\n- `npm run typecheck`: passed for root and viewer.\n- `npm run build`: passed.",
